  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1257 AM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nThe synoptic pattern through Friday will feature a 500mb ridge \nacross the southwestern U.S. and downstream troughing across the \nGreat Lakes/Ohio Valley. The trough will become reinforced as a \nshortwave diving south out of Manitoba pushes southeast into the \nGreat Lakes through Wednesday night. This will allow a cold front to \ndrop south into the Ohio Valley Wednesday night into Thursday. \nBefore that occurs, a warm front lifts north towards us tonight into \nWednesday morning which may ignite a few showers or isolated storms \n(most likely elevated) across southeast Missouri. A greater chance \nfor showers and storms exists Wednesday evening into Thursday \nmorning as the cold front slips south across the area. Most of the \nCAMs struggle to generate much instability Wednesday afternoon and \nevening (with the exception of the 3km NAM), but if we can tap into \nsufficient MLCAPE then a few strong to severe storms could be \npossible as deep layer shear increases to around 25 kts Wednesday \nevening.\n\nIn the wake of the fropa on Thursday, a reinforcement of the lower \nhumidity surges down across the area Thursday night through \nSaturday. As we head into the weekend, the ridge across the \nsouthwest U.S. begins to build eastward and by Sunday into Monday it \nwill encompass the entire southern U.S. 850mb temps rise into the 20-\n22C range, helping to boost temperatures above 90 again. Some \nguidance suggests we may get quite toasty in the mid to even upper \n90s early next week. Dew points creep up as well, back into the \nupper 60s to lower 70s, which would allow heat index readings to \npossibly exceed 100 again. While LREF probabilities of exceeding 90 \npeak Sunday and Monday (>90 percent chance) they remain quite high \n(75-90%) even into mid next week. Latest ensemble guidance suggests \nthe ridge may remain in place through the entirety of next week even.\n\nThe probability of receiving at least 0.25\" is only 15-25% through \nnext Monday according to the latest run of the LREF. Any one \nlocation is more likely to remain dry through the next 7 days then \nto receive appreciable rainfall.",
